// Relativization / partition-safety, `previous` operator, keyed by a CONTEXT
// field (`tenant_id`).
//
// A Read is permitted iff THIS tenant's immediately preceding own event (within
// 1h) was a Login. The event schema pins `tenant_id = context.tenant_id`
// universally (see event.dwschema) with NO scope pin, so `previous` means "this
// tenant's previous event" and the ONLY thing confining evaluation is the
// context-field pin.
//
// Principal AND resource are held constant (alice / gw1) across every event, so
// neither scope entity can distinguish "mine" from "foreign" — only the tenant
// context field can. The differential pair for tenant t1's Read at @3:
//   trace_1: t1 Login, T2 Transfer (foreign tenant, same principal+resource), t1 Read.
//   trace_2: t1 Login,                                                         t1 Read.
// Under tenant-local semantics the t2 Transfer is invisible to t1, so t1's
// previous own event is the Login in BOTH traces -> PERMIT in both. Under
// (incorrect) global semantics -- or a bug that keyed on scope (principal and
// resource are identical everywhere), or that mis-resolved the context pin --
// trace_1's previous event would be the t2 Transfer -> deny, diverging from
// trace_2. The context pin is what excludes it.

permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Read",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    previous within 1h Drupe::Action::"Login"::request{ input.user: context.input.user }
};
